{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-71209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Given that the point $P(-2,2,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}={x}^{2}y+2x-y$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(-2,2,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(-2,2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(-2,2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-1 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90798699906b169dbc2b79706dc3e351b5de0f03ace3a8f06e0d0c33d09f0603"} {"question" "q-71209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-5\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-3\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=4y-5\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=6y-3\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71209" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-3\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=-1.$ Given that the point $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=\\frac{x(y-x)}{2x(y+1)}$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-1,0) & =\\frac{(-1)(0-(-1))}{2(-1)(0+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-1,0) & ={f}_{y}(-1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}y \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-1,0,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1 \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=\\frac{1}{2}y+\\frac{1}{2}$ (or $y-2z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,-2⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =2\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $⟨0,2,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90798699906b169dbc2b79706dc3e351b5de0f03ace3a8f06e0d0c33d09f0603"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,-2,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,2,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-71234" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,2,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Given that the point $P(4,-2,5)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=y\\sqrt{x}$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(4,-2,5)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(4,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)} & =(-2)\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =-2(2) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(4,-2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(4,-2)}(x-4) \\\\ z-5 & =-4(x-4) \\\\ z & =-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(4,-2,5)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90798699906b169dbc2b79706dc3e351b5de0f03ace3a8f06e0d0c33d09f0603"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-2x+15\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+9\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+21\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=2x+9\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132585" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}y=-2 \\\\ z=-4x+21\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=2.$ Given that the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90798699906b169dbc2b79706dc3e351b5de0f03ace3a8f06e0d0c33d09f0603"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y+\\pi \\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}"] ["math" "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132593" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $x=1.$ Given that the point $P(1,0,-1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{y}=x{e}^{xy}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$, which of the following gives a tangent vector to $C$ at $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(1,0).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(1,0) & =1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 0}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,0) & ={f}_{y}(1,0)(y-0) \\\\ z-(-1) & =(-1)y \\\\ z & =-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,0,-1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1 \\\\ z=-y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=-y-1$ (or $y+z+1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $-j+k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "90798699906b169dbc2b79706dc3e351b5de0f03ace3a8f06e0d0c33d09f0603"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,j+k"] ["math" "-j+2\\,k"] ["math" "-j+k"] ["math" "2j-3k"] ["math" "j+k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"} {"question" "q-132604" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-j+k"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068975/state.json" "file_sha256" "6632622d57c90c7b1799fed105629cd08de046356fe466d6bbe535b3b3354f03"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2329 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
